# 🛡️ Granting Administrative Privileges

The two system administrators were granted administrative privileges by adding them to the **sudo** group.

The following commands were executed:

```bash
sudo usermod -aG sudo alice
sudo usermod -aG sudo bob
```

The configuration was verified using:

```bash
id alice
id bob
```

The output confirmed that both users were members of the **sudo** group and could execute administrative commands using `sudo`.

![Sudo group membership](../../screenshots/lab-05/09-sudo-group.png)

---

# ✅ Verifying Administrative Access

The administrator account **alice** was used to verify that administrative privileges were correctly configured.

A new login session was started:

```bash
su - alice
```

The following command was executed:

```bash
sudo whoami
```

After entering Alice's password, the command returned:

```text
root
```

This confirmed that Alice was authorized to execute commands with superuser privileges through `sudo`.

---

# 🚫 Verifying Restricted Administrative Access

The developer account **charlie** was then tested.

A new login session was started:

```bash
su - charlie
```

The following command was executed:

```bash
sudo whoami
```

The operation failed because the user was not a member of the **sudo** group.

The system displayed a message indicating that Charlie was not authorized to execute administrative commands.

This verification demonstrates the **Principle of Least Privilege**, ensuring that elevated privileges are granted only to authorized administrators.

![Sudo access denied](../../screenshots/lab-05/10-sudo-access.png)

